==========================================
DAY 11 NOTES — Archiving & Compression
Date: 08-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- tar bundles files together; it does NOT compress by itself
- gzip is the compression step, usually combined with tar in one command
- tar -czf = create + gzip + filename → the most common archive command
- tar -tzf lets you PEEK inside an archive before extracting (important for untrusted files)
- .tar.gz is the Linux-standard format; .zip is better for cross-platform (Windows) sharing
- gzip file.txt REPLACES the original with file.txt.gz (use -k to keep both)
- -C flag lets you extract an archive to a specific folder instead of the current one

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] tar -cf / tar -czf
[ ] tar -tzf (listing contents)
[ ] tar -xzf (extracting)
[ ] gzip / gunzip
[ ] zip -r / unzip

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ tar -czf backup.tar.gz practice/
> 

$ tar -tzf backup.tar.gz
> 

$ ls -lh backup.tar backup.tar.gz
> 


